{"audio":{"src":"https://pub-13abeb101e2945d2a7f60234f6faf52e.r2.dev/audio/ovi-english-b1-2026-07-14-why-villains-are-interesting-psychology-b1-0efaf6be0d36.mp3","durationSec":210.624,"cues":[{"start":0,"end":6.6,"text":"K. Maja Krakowiak and Mary Beth Oliver compared stories with good, bad, or morally mixed characters."},{"start":7.54,"end":9.5,"text":"The bad characters were liked the least."},{"start":10.3,"end":14.8,"text":"However, their stories were just as suspenseful and mentally engaging as the others."},{"start":15.48,"end":21.44,"text":"This gives us the first answer: liking a character and paying attention to a character are different."},{"start":22.08,"end":26.56,"text":"A villain can be unpleasant and still make a story difficult to leave."},{"start":27.82,"end":32.92,"text":"Psychologist Arne Öhman and his team asked people to find one different face in a grid."},{"start":33.82,"end":40.12,"text":"Across five experiments, people found threatening faces faster and more accurately than friendly faces."},{"start":40.88,"end":44.92,"text":"This was not a study of villains, so it is only one part of the answer."},{"start":45.78,"end":49.78,"text":"It shows a useful part of attention: possible danger can get priority."},{"start":50.76,"end":56.88,"text":"A fictional villain adds plans and choices, so we keep watching for what that person might do next."},{"start":57.36,"end":62.56,"text":"Psychologist Coltan Scrivner developed a 24-question Morbid Curiosity Scale."},{"start":63.3,"end":71.22,"text":"It measures interest in four areas: dangerous minds, violence between people, injured bodies, and paranormal danger."},{"start":72.12,"end":77,"text":"In the final experiment, people chose between dark and neutral pictures or descriptions."},{"start":78.3,"end":81.86,"text":"People with higher scores were more likely to choose the dark information."},{"start":82.92,"end":87.32,"text":"Villains fit one area especially well: the minds of dangerous people."},{"start":88,"end":91.32,"text":"We want to know the motive, plan, or hidden history."},{"start":92.18,"end":102.86,"text":"Paul Rozin and his team studied 29 experiences that usually feel unpleasant, including fear, sadness, pain, strong tastes, and physical exhaustion."},{"start":103.66,"end":106.86,"text":"Many people said they enjoyed some of them when the experience was safe."},{"start":108.06,"end":110.62,"text":"The researchers called this benign masochism."},{"start":111.36,"end":113.72,"text":"A frightening story can work in a similar way."},{"start":114.16,"end":118.22,"text":"Your body reacts, but your mind knows you can pause the film or close the book."},{"start":118.92,"end":123.04,"text":"The danger feels real enough to be exciting, but not real enough to hurt you."},{"start":123.8,"end":125.9,"text":"Some villains are not simply cruel."},{"start":126.62,"end":132.16,"text":"They may love one person, protect a group, or regret one choice while continuing to cause harm."},{"start":132.16,"end":139.7,"text":"This creates moral ambiguity: we can understand part of the character without approving of the action."},{"start":140.82,"end":152.28,"text":"In Krakowiak and Oliver's study, morally mixed characters were liked less than good characters, yet their stories were equally suspenseful, engaging, and enjoyable."},{"start":153.26,"end":162.14,"text":"When several motives are possible, the audience keeps testing predictions: Will the villain change, repeat the harm, or surprise us?"},{"start":162.14,"end":164.94,"text":"This uncertainty creates suspense."},{"start":166.26,"end":168.42,"text":"Different villains use different combinations."},{"start":169.22,"end":174,"text":"Darth Vader brings threat, but his past also creates a question about change."},{"start":174.98,"end":182.3,"text":"Iago in Othello tells the audience about his plan, so suspense comes from waiting to see who will understand the trap."},{"start":183.42,"end":191.2,"text":"Frankenstein's creature causes harm but also describes rejection and loneliness, which creates moral ambiguity."},{"start":191.2,"end":197.62,"text":"Fun fact: Mary Shelley never gives the creature a personal name; Frankenstein is the creator."},{"start":198.76,"end":202.96,"text":"When a villain stays in your mind, ask four questions: What is the threat?"},{"start":203.68,"end":204.76,"text":"What information is missing?"},{"start":205.68,"end":207.18,"text":"Why does the story feel safe?"},{"start":207.96,"end":209.86,"text":"Which motive is difficult to predict?"}],"words":[{"text":"k","start":0,"end":0.18},{"text":"maya","start":0.34,"end":0.48},{"text":"krakowiak","start":0.48,"end":1.26},{"text":"and","start":1.26,"end":1.6},{"text":"mary","start":1.6,"end":1.76},{"text":"beth","start":1.76,"end":2.04},{"text":"oliver","start":2.04,"end":2.3},{"text":"compared","start":2.3,"end":3.1},{"text":"stories","start":3.1,"end":3.82},{"text":"with","start":3.82,"end":4.1},{"text":"good","start":4.1,"end":4.42},{"text":"bad","start":4.7,"end":4.96},{"text":"or","start":5.24,"end":5.38},{"text":"morally","start":5.38,"end":5.72},{"text":"mixed","start":5.72,"end":6.1},{"text":"characters","start":6.1,"end":6.6},{"text":"the","start":7.54,"end":7.66},{"text":"bad","start":7.66,"end":7.9},{"text":"characters","start":7.9,"end":8.36},{"text":"we","start":8.36,"end":8.56},{"text":"are","start":8.56,"end":8.76},{"text":"liked","start":8.76,"end":8.96},{"text":"the","start":8.96,"end":9.3},{"text":"least","start":9.3,"end":9.5},{"text":"however","start":10.3,"end":10.5},{"text":"their","start":10.72,"end":10.84},{"text":"stories","start":10.84,"end":11.26},{"text":"we","start":11.26,"end":11.35},{"text":"are","start":11.35,"end":11.44},{"text":"just","start":11.44,"end":11.78},{"text":"as","start":11.78,"end":11.98},{"text":"suspenseful","start":11.98,"end":12.74},{"text":"and","start":12.74,"end":13.06},{"text":"mentally","start":13.06,"end":13.44},{"text":"engaging","start":13.44,"end":13.84},{"t